WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:03.100
Film Courage: The primary goal of every story
is to…

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:04.840
Michael Hauge: Illicit emotion.

00:00:04.840 --> 00:00:06.820
Film Courage: Illicit emotion.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:07.820
Okay.

00:00:07.820 --> 00:00:09.660
And if it’s too stale.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.540
Let’s say the writer thinks there’s emotion
and it’s going somewhere, but the reader

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:19.349
is not getting the emotion that the reader
intends, where is the breakdown there?

00:00:19.349 --> 00:00:24.039
Michael Hauge: The breakdown is definitely
with the writer.

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:29.659
It’s not an audience’s or a reader’s
job to feel something, something that you

00:00:29.659 --> 00:00:31.039
have to illicit.

00:00:31.039 --> 00:00:36.899
So it means you haven’t created sufficient
conflict in your story to illicit emotion

00:00:36.899 --> 00:00:43.090
or you haven’t created a premise (a concept
of a story) you haven’t structured in such

00:00:43.090 --> 00:00:48.540
a way that the concept makes any sense or
can be emotionally involving because you’ve

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:52.980
given the reader nothing to route for or you
haven’t created empathy with the hero or

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:53.980
whatever it might be.

00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.829
But it’s your job to illicit emotion.

00:00:56.829 --> 00:01:02.239
The emotion has to come naturally from the
reader and audience because of what you’ve

00:01:02.239 --> 00:01:03.530
done.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:08.300
They have to be participating in the story
you have created.

00:01:08.300 --> 00:01:14.690
That is why for example empathy is so important
for the hero (for you to create for your hero)

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:22.390
because a story is a participatory experience
for the audience or the reader.

00:01:22.390 --> 00:01:24.520
We become that character.

00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:27.560
We want to experience that emotion as they
do.

00:01:27.560 --> 00:01:32.220
We don’t go to the movies because it’s
interesting to watch people do things.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.030
It’s emotional for us to get to do them.

00:01:35.030 --> 00:01:36.790
We become Jason Bourne.

00:01:36.790 --> 00:01:40.790
We become Rose as she falls in love Jack and
is on the TITANIC.

00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:48.000
We become the woman, the astronaut whose out
in space in GRAVITY.

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.120
We are part of it.

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.170
We are participating in the action, in the
events of that story.

00:01:53.170 --> 00:01:58.700
But we have no basis, no way to get into the
story if we don’t on a subconscious level

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:00.690
become the hero of that story.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:05.890
And that’s what the empathy and the identification
you create at the beginning is going to do.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:10.890
So if you’ve created a story with an empathetic
hero, so we participate, giving them a clear

00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:17.080
goal, and created enough logical believable
obstacles to overcome, then the emotion will

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:22.301
grow organically out of your audience because
they’re in it and you’ve created conflict,

00:02:22.301 --> 00:02:23.710
the conflict that will do that.

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:28.260
Film Courage: What about the writer that just
puts incident after incident of conflict,

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.630
but then the story just falls flat?

00:02:30.630 --> 00:02:32.210
What’s it missing?

00:02:32.210 --> 00:02:35.390
Michael Hauge: Well it could be missing any
number of things.

00:02:35.390 --> 00:02:40.500
But as I said earlier, all roads lead to the
outer motivation.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:46.181
And so if all you’re doing is stringing
together obstacles to overcome…and there

00:02:46.181 --> 00:02:51.560
is a very similar mistake and that is people
who think comedy is one funny thing after

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:52.660
another happening.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:56.020
That’s not what a story is.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:03.740
A story is the sequence of events experienced
by a hero who is pursuing a very specific

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:04.740
goal.

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:08.440
So if you’re just throwing out one obstacle
after another but we don’t care about the

00:03:08.440 --> 00:03:13.600
hero or it’s not clear why the hero is doing
this or what they’re trying to achieve or

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:20.950
if the obstacles are illogical or if the pace
of the story lags or if everything is at a

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:25.900
very high level of emotion and you are not
creating sort of peaks and valleys to the

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:31.630
emotional level so you have big moments and
quiet moments connect and moments of separation

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:32.700
and so on.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:39.310
There are those things you’ve got to do
in addition to throwing a bunch of car chases

00:03:39.310 --> 00:03:44.510
and fight scenes up on the screen which many
people have tried and those movies don’t

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:45.510
succeed.

00:03:45.510 --> 00:03:52.080
If it’s just car chases and fight scenes,
it’s just a series of videos really.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:55.780
But it’s not a story or a movie that people
are going to be involved in.

00:03:55.780 --> 00:03:59.430
Film Courage: So there has to be a resting
point.

00:03:59.430 --> 00:04:00.430
You said peaks and valleys.

00:04:00.430 --> 00:04:02.260
So at some point it has to kind of drop?

00:04:02.260 --> 00:04:04.030
Michael Hauge: Well it has to alternate.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.830
It’s not a resting point as if you stay
there forever.

00:04:07.830 --> 00:04:11.520
But you don’t…it’s like in a comedy…you
don’t want joke, joke, joke.

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.500
There’s got to be a serious moment or two.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.430
And look at any great comedy, look at even
any decent comedy.

00:04:18.430 --> 00:04:22.789
There is going to be something very funny,
but then there is going to be something touching

00:04:22.789 --> 00:04:27.870
or sad or romantic or there is going to be
a deeper connection between the characters

00:04:27.870 --> 00:04:32.919
or there is going to be something more exciting
or there is going to be something softer.

00:04:32.919 --> 00:04:38.909
You want to vary the peaks and valleys, the
emotional level and pitch of the story as

00:04:38.909 --> 00:04:43.940
we go steadily toward the finish line that
the hero is trying to reach.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.290
So the obstacles need to get bigger.

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:50.789
They need to come closer together as it moves
forward so the pace will accelerate but if

00:04:50.789 --> 00:04:56.810
there is only obstacles, one right after the
other with no chance for us to catch our breath,

00:04:56.810 --> 00:05:01.740
no chance to anticipate what might be coming,
no chance to explain what is going on and

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:08.439
no chance to get closer or deeper to the characters,
it’s going to be a very, very shallow story

00:05:08.439 --> 00:05:11.659
with very limited emotional involvement or
appeal.

00:05:11.659 --> 00:05:11.929
Question for the Viewers: Have you ever walked
out of a movie theater or asked for a refund?

